    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d621">

                <pb facs="0685" n="590" sameAs="frus:frus1936v04/pg590"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/6144: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bingham</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-04-18T13:00:00+00:00">April 18, 1936—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 18—9 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>212. Your 123, April 11, 2 p.m. In the absence of Atherton, substance of the
                    Department’s views was communicated informally to Orde yesterday by
                        Johnson.<anchor n="96" xml:id="d621-f1"/> Orde made no comment except to say
                    that they were still waiting for advices from the Treasury in the matter and he
                    reiterated that it was not Leith-Ross who was conducting the negotiations, that
                    Leith-Ross had only given advice.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bingham</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d621-f1" n="96" xml:id="d621-f1-content">
                <p>Herschel V. Johnson, First Secretary of Embassy in the United Kingdom.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
